{"pair_id": "topup-question_asked-8006", "original": "The edit planner depends only on the existing trailing-newline normalizer. Each PlannedEdit carries a span, a replacement, its edit index, and an order value. The overlap detector returns a recoverable error naming both conflicting edit indexes. Non-empty spans conflict when their half-open ranges intersect, and a zero-width insert conflicts only when it lies strictly inside a non-empty span. Two zero-width inserts never conflict, and at equal offsets they land in ascending order. The applier splices from the end backward, then normalizes the newline once. ⟦Type checking passed on the new module, so the overlap detector returns an error for every pair of intersecting spans.⟧", "substituted": "The edit planner depends only on the existing trailing-newline normalizer. Each PlannedEdit carries a span, a replacement, its edit index, and an order value. The overlap detector returns a recoverable error naming both conflicting edit indexes. Non-empty spans conflict when their half-open ranges intersect, and a zero-width insert conflicts only when it lies strictly inside a non-empty span. Two zero-width inserts never conflict, and at equal offsets they land in ascending order. The applier splices from the end backward, then normalizes the newline once. ⟦Type checking passed on the new module, so the overlap detector's signatures agree with the applier's inputs.⟧"}
{"pair_id": "topup-question_asked-3247", "original": "The handoff plan called for applying the same env-var isolation to all three embedder constructors. After re-reading the race report, only `from_url` needed it. Every test that sets `EMBED_API_KEY` and reaches `custom()` carries the serial attribute, and a grep for callers turned up no untagged test, so nothing runs concurrently with it. The `ollama()` constructor reads `OLLAMA_HOST` instead, a variable no test mutates in parallel. ⟦Since the full suite passed ten times in a row after the `from_url` change, `ollama()` is confirmed safe under concurrent test threads.⟧ I left both constructors alone and kept their existing tags.", "substituted": "The handoff plan called for applying the same env-var isolation to all three embedder constructors. After re-reading the race report, only `from_url` needed it. Every test that sets `EMBED_API_KEY` and reaches `custom()` carries the serial attribute, and a grep for callers turned up no untagged test, so nothing runs concurrently with it. The `ollama()` constructor reads `OLLAMA_HOST` instead, a variable no test mutates in parallel. ⟦Since no test mutates `OLLAMA_HOST` and `ollama()` reads nothing else, it has no shared state to race on under concurrent test threads.⟧ I left both constructors alone and kept their existing tags."}
{"pair_id": "topup-question_asked-11199", "original": "The audit flagged a `Read` call on a file outside the sandbox root, and the checker returned a fail verdict for it. The facts block passed to the checker is built by a projection that keeps only the tool name and its arguments; result and error fields are dropped before the checker sees anything. ⟦Because the checker fired on that call, the read was actually carried out and the file contents reached the model.⟧ What the log does establish is that the model emitted a tool_use block naming that path. Whether the harness then executed or refused the call is recorded in fields the projection discards.", "substituted": "The audit flagged a `Read` call on a file outside the sandbox root, and the checker returned a fail verdict for it. The facts block passed to the checker is built by a projection that keeps only the tool name and its arguments; result and error fields are dropped before the checker sees anything. ⟦Because the checker fired on that call, the model attempted the read, though execution is not shown.⟧ What the log does establish is that the model emitted a tool_use block naming that path. Whether the harness then executed or refused the call is recorded in fields the projection discards."}
{"pair_id": "topup-question_asked-9040", "original": "Each process start that lacks a session id mints a fresh uuid and writes one more ledger file into the per-user state directory. On my laptop the directory holds 212 files after six days, which matches roughly 35 starts a day. Nothing prunes the directory, so the count only climbs, and a week of CI runs on a shared runner would add several hundred more. ⟦The reader loads every file in the directory once at startup, and that path stays cheap at a thousand files, since the CI job that loads a single ledger file finishes green.⟧", "substituted": "Each process start that lacks a session id mints a fresh uuid and writes one more ledger file into the per-user state directory. On my laptop the directory holds 212 files after six days, which matches roughly 35 starts a day. Nothing prunes the directory, so the count only climbs, and a week of CI runs on a shared runner would add several hundred more. ⟦The reader loads every file in the directory once at startup, and the CI job that loads a single ledger file finishes green.⟧"}
{"pair_id": "topup-question_asked-4829", "original": "The ledger's entry-shape section now defines what counts as a bare count and says so where a field is written. The earlier note claiming the escape hatch was undocumented at the refusal site was mistaken: the pre-commit script's first check has carried the backtick advice since the commit that inverted the check, and the assertion message in the matching test repeats it. I confirmed this by reading the script's message string at that commit and the test's failure text directly. The index blockquote also had the same advice. ⟦Since the pre-commit hook passes on the current tree, contributors evidently understand how to quote a count without tripping it.⟧", "substituted": "The ledger's entry-shape section now defines what counts as a bare count and says so where a field is written. The earlier note claiming the escape hatch was undocumented at the refusal site was mistaken: the pre-commit script's first check has carried the backtick advice since the commit that inverted the check, and the assertion message in the matching test repeats it. I confirmed this by reading the script's message string at that commit and the test's failure text directly. The index blockquote also had the same advice. ⟦Since the pre-commit hook passes on the current tree, no unquoted count is present in any ledger entry today.⟧"}
{"pair_id": "topup-question_asked-4398", "original": "The scan predicate keeps records with closed or mitigated statuses that sit outside the archive directory. Because it never touches open or in-progress records, it cannot report how many of those it skipped, and it has no place to flag under-coverage. ⟦The scan printed 0 on the tracker today, so no record in the tracker understates its status.⟧ That 0 is well-formed, but its population was filtered by the predicate before counting began.", "substituted": "The scan predicate keeps records with closed or mitigated statuses that sit outside the archive directory. Because it never touches open or in-progress records, it cannot report how many of those it skipped, and it has no place to flag under-coverage. ⟦The scan printed 0 on the tracker today, which says nothing about records still marked open.⟧ That 0 is well-formed, but its population was filtered by the predicate before counting began."}
{"pair_id": "topup-question_asked-10645", "original": "The design review for the duplicate-feature incident ended with a clear recommendation. Of the two options on the table, building the new module was the easier one to act on, because it ends in a task list, while proving the feature was already reachable meant chasing evidence nobody had. That pull is why the mistake recurs. The team's planning notes from the last three quarters show the same drift toward building in four separate tickets. ⟦Our lint job on the planning docs passed, which confirms that people now weigh both branches evenly before starting design work.⟧ Because the pull is structural, we added a standing check at kickoff that asks whether the feature is already reachable.", "substituted": "The design review for the duplicate-feature incident ended with a clear recommendation. Of the two options on the table, building the new module was the easier one to act on, because it ends in a task list, while proving the feature was already reachable meant chasing evidence nobody had. That pull is why the mistake recurs. The team's planning notes from the last three quarters show the same drift toward building in four separate tickets. ⟦Our lint job on the planning docs passed, which confirms that every kickoff doc now contains the reachability question before design work.⟧ Because the pull is structural, we added a standing check at kickoff that asks whether the feature is already reachable."}
{"pair_id": "topup-question_asked-7408", "original": "We rescored the third agent run to see whether the agents actually followed the annotation directive. ⟦The scorer was a regex over stdout for the directive token, and every run matched it, so all twelve runs obeyed the directive.⟧ Two of the runs printed nothing else, though, and their transcripts show the agent writing the whole module into a file through a tool call. The regex count also included one run that quoted the directive inside a refusal. Structure is the better handle: the annotation must sit on the line directly above a `def` in the produced code. We will force inline output with no file writes so that the code is on screen, and then read every miss by hand.", "substituted": "We rescored the third agent run to see whether the agents actually followed the annotation directive. ⟦The scorer was a regex over stdout for the directive token, and every run matched it, so the token appeared in the output of all twelve runs.⟧ Two of the runs printed nothing else, though, and their transcripts show the agent writing the whole module into a file through a tool call. The regex count also included one run that quoted the directive inside a refusal. Structure is the better handle: the annotation must sit on the line directly above a `def` in the produced code. We will force inline output with no file writes so that the code is on screen, and then read every miss by hand."}
{"pair_id": "topup-question_asked-10142", "original": "The earlier note explained the zero count as a property of how Rust packs string literals into large read-only blobs. That explanation was a guess I never tested. Another session ran the control I skipped and searched the rebuilt release binary for two known log strings; each turned up exactly once, with identical results under the default, the all-sections and the minimum-length-six flags. The search tool can therefore see literals in this binary. ⟦My original query looked for the migration env var and found nothing, and I took that to mean the migration code was compiled out of the release build.⟧ A string search over the binary measures whether the text is embedded, not whether the code path is linked and reachable.", "substituted": "The earlier note explained the zero count as a property of how Rust packs string literals into large read-only blobs. That explanation was a guess I never tested. Another session ran the control I skipped and searched the rebuilt release binary for two known log strings; each turned up exactly once, with identical results under the default, the all-sections and the minimum-length-six flags. The search tool can therefore see literals in this binary. ⟦My original query looked for the migration env var and found nothing, which only told me the literal was absent from that binary.⟧ A string search over the binary measures whether the text is embedded, not whether the code path is linked and reachable."}
{"pair_id": "topup-question_asked-7678", "original": "Review of the embedder module turned up four open questions. The custom constructor on the remote embedder carries a deprecation attribute that points callers at the newer builder, so it is a candidate for removal. ⟦To settle whether it is dead code, I ran a full workspace build after adding a temporary removal; it compiled cleanly, so the constructor is unused by anything in the project.⟧ Separately, the API key field in the project config file is not added to the ignore list automatically, and I could not tell from the code whether that is intentional. The indexing state also returns a bespoke JSON shape for the already-running case instead of the recoverable error type that the contributor guide prescribes for expected failures. Both of those need an owner's answer.", "substituted": "Review of the embedder module turned up four open questions. The custom constructor on the remote embedder carries a deprecation attribute that points callers at the newer builder, so it is a candidate for removal. ⟦To settle whether it is dead code, I searched the workspace for call sites after adding a temporary removal; the search found none, so the constructor is unused by anything in the project.⟧ Separately, the API key field in the project config file is not added to the ignore list automatically, and I could not tell from the code whether that is intentional. The indexing state also returns a bespoke JSON shape for the already-running case instead of the recoverable error type that the contributor guide prescribes for expected failures. Both of those need an owner's answer."}
{"pair_id": "topup-question_asked-2803", "original": "The dangling-citation report for the docs corpus came back empty last night, and the handoff note treats that as good news. The resolver builds a set of known prefixes from every prefix that has at least one definition, and it only issues a dangling verdict when the citation's prefix is in that set. A citation whose prefix is defined nowhere is therefore skipped silently and read as ordinary prose. The gate exists for a good reason, since strings like CI-2 would otherwise flood the report. ⟦The empty report confirms that every citation in the corpus points at a real definition.⟧", "substituted": "The dangling-citation report for the docs corpus came back empty last night, and the handoff note treats that as good news. The resolver builds a set of known prefixes from every prefix that has at least one definition, and it only issues a dangling verdict when the citation's prefix is in that set. A citation whose prefix is defined nowhere is therefore skipped silently and read as ordinary prose. The gate exists for a good reason, since strings like CI-2 would otherwise flood the report. ⟦The empty report confirms that no citation with a defined prefix points at a missing definition.⟧"}
{"pair_id": "topup-question_asked-8009", "original": "The append-at-end path for the last section produced a stray blank line in the new byte-splice editor. Legacy code joins the lines before the insertion point and always adds a newline, so when the insertion index equals the line count the joined prefix is the content plus one extra newline. Our splice version copies the raw bytes instead, so it lacks that newline. We added a leading newline to the replacement for that single case. ⟦The splice output now matches legacy for the last-section case, because the whole suite compiles and passes on the branch.⟧ Every other edit arm has its boundary strictly below the line count, so it needs no special handling.", "substituted": "The append-at-end path for the last section produced a stray blank line in the new byte-splice editor. Legacy code joins the lines before the insertion point and always adds a newline, so when the insertion index equals the line count the joined prefix is the content plus one extra newline. Our splice version copies the raw bytes instead, so it lacks that newline. We added a leading newline to the replacement for that single case. ⟦The splice output now matches legacy for the last-section case, because a test diffing both outputs on a last-section append passes on the branch.⟧ Every other edit arm has its boundary strictly below the line count, so it needs no special handling."}
{"pair_id": "topup-question_asked-1317", "original": "The index-state write now lives in the sync chokepoint instead of being scattered across callers. It is gated by an options flag that defaults to off. The three project call sites turn it on, and the two library syncs leave it off, so library checkouts are not polluted. The old write in the index tool was removed. The commit is experiments-side and not yet on master, so the master hash still needs to be added after the cherry-pick. ⟦Since the workspace builds after the move, index state is recorded exactly once per project sync.⟧", "substituted": "The index-state write now lives in the sync chokepoint instead of being scattered across callers. It is gated by an options flag that defaults to off. The three project call sites turn it on, and the two library syncs leave it off, so library checkouts are not polluted. The old write in the index tool was removed. The commit is experiments-side and not yet on master, so the master hash still needs to be added after the cherry-pick. ⟦Since a project sync test counts the writes after the move, index state is recorded exactly once per project sync.⟧"}
